      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "To which of the following sets does the number $\\frac{13}{4}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we note that $\\frac{13}{4}=\\frac{13}{4}$ is a positive rational number that is not an integer.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}$ contains all real numbers. Therefore, $\\frac{13}{4}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$ contains the nonnegative real numbers. Therefore, $\\frac{13}{4}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^{+}$ contains the positive rational numbers. Therefore, $\\frac{13}{4}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{+}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$ contains the nonpositive real numbers. Therefore, $\\frac{13}{4}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\nTherefore, select $\\mathbb{R}$, $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$, $\\mathbb{Q}^{+}$ only.",
