      ":question/worked-solution": "The radicand $106$ is not a perfect square, so $-\\sqrt{106}$ is a negative irrational number.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$ contains the nonnegative real numbers. Therefore, $-\\sqrt{106}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}^{-}$ contains the negative real numbers. Therefore, $-\\sqrt{106}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}^{-}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}$ contains all real numbers. Therefore, $-\\sqrt{106}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$ contains the nonpositive real numbers. Therefore, $-\\sqrt{106}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\nTherefore, select $\\mathbb{R}^{-}$, $\\mathbb{R}$, $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$ only.",
