      ":question/worked-solution": "The radicand $57$ is not a perfect square, so $-\\sqrt{57}$ is a negative irrational number.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{N}$ contains the positive integers (natural numbers). Therefore, $-\\sqrt{57}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{N}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^{+}$ contains the positive rational numbers. Therefore, $-\\sqrt{57}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^{+}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}$ contains all real numbers. Therefore, $-\\sqrt{57}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}_0^{+}$ contains the nonnegative integers. Therefore, $-\\sqrt{57}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{Z}_0^{+}$.\n\nTherefore, select $\\mathbb{R}$ only.",
