      ":question/worked-solution": "The number $\\frac{1}{\\pi}$ is a positive irrational number. It is not a rational number.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}$ contains all real numbers. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{\\pi}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$ contains the nonnegative real numbers. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{\\pi}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{C}$ contains all complex numbers. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{\\pi}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{C}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}^{+}$ contains the positive real numbers. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{\\pi}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}^{+}$.\n\nTherefore, select $\\mathbb{R}$, $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$, $\\mathbb{C}$, $\\mathbb{R}^{+}$ only.",
