      ":question/worked-solution": "The radicand $11$ is not a perfect square, so $\\sqrt{11}$ is a positive irrational number.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{C}$ contains all complex numbers. Therefore, $\\sqrt{11}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{C}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}^{-}$ contains the negative integers. Therefore, $\\sqrt{11}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{Z}^{-}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{N}$ contains the positive integers (natural numbers). Therefore, $\\sqrt{11}$ does not belong to $\\mathbb{N}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}^{+}$ contains the positive real numbers. Therefore, $\\sqrt{11}$ belongs to $\\mathbb{R}^{+}$.\n\nTherefore, select $\\mathbb{C}$, $\\mathbb{R}^{+}$ only.",
